# Line endings and text encoding, for issue #26.
#
# Every tracked text file is stored with a line feed and checked out with a line
# feed, on every platform. `text=auto` lets git decide which files are text by
# the same rule it always has, and `eol=lf` overrides whatever a personal
# `core.autocrlf` setting would otherwise do to the checkout. Without this line
# a clone on Windows carries a carriage return on every line of every file, the
# formatter names every source file at once, and a fixture whose bytes have to
# be exact is quietly a different fixture than the one that was written.
#
# The encoding is UTF-8, which is what git stores and what the toolchain reads,
# and it is not declared here because there is no attribute that would make it
# refusable. `working-tree-encoding` is the neighbouring attribute and it exists
# to hold a file in something other than UTF-8, which is the opposite of what
# this repository wants. The guard on the tracked bytes is what stands behind
# the encoding, not this file.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary fixtures, excluded by path so that nothing is normalised on the way in
# or out. A media fixture is a sequence of bytes a decoder is asked about, and a
# byte changed by a checkout makes it a different question.
#
# The suffixes below are the ones this repository expects to hold. Each is
# declared rather than left to the content heuristic, because the heuristic
# reads the first few kilobytes for a zero byte, and a short payload that
# happens to contain none would be normalised without anybody noticing.
# internal/textbytes reads this file for these patterns rather than holding a
# second copy of the list, so adding a suffix here is the whole change.
*.bin binary
*.gif binary
*.gz binary
*.h264 binary
*.ico binary
*.ivf binary
*.jpg binary
*.mp4 binary
*.ogg binary
*.opus binary
*.pcap binary
*.pdf binary
*.png binary
*.webm binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.zip binary
